course: dictionary
lesson: 1879
title: Words for people at work
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A colleague is a person you work with, especially in a job. The words around it are clues. «At work there is usually a boss who decides what needs doing, colleagues who do it alongside them, meetings to agree plans.» The boss decides, and the colleagues do the work with them, so colleagues are the people in the same job.
explain: «Colleagues are the people you work alongside, often for more hours a week than you see your own family; some become friends and some get on your nerves.» Alongside means side by side, so a colleague is someone beside you at work. Some colleagues are friends and some are not, and the sentence tells you both.
explain: Teachers have colleagues too. A teacher's colleagues are the other teachers in the same school. To check your meaning, swap it in: "people I work with" fits the sentence, so you have understood colleague.
fact: A colleague is a person you work with, especially in a job or profession.
fact: One person is a colleague, and more than one are colleagues.
fact: A teacher's colleagues are other teachers.
fact: Colleague is a polite, slightly formal word, so you will see it in news stories and letters.
fact: Clues around the word, such as boss, meetings and work, help you understand it.
exercise: «At work there is usually a boss who decides what needs doing.» Who decides what needs doing? | The meetings | *The boss | The colleagues | The emails
exercise: Ms Patel says, «My colleagues and I are planning the school trip.» Who is planning the trip? | The bus driver alone | *Ms Patel and the other teachers | The children only | Ms Patel's parents
exercise: «Colleagues are the people you work alongside.» What is a colleague? | A pet that helps on the farm | A member of your family | *A person you work with | A friend you only see at the weekend
test: «Some become friends and some get on your nerves.» Which is true about colleagues? | Colleagues are always your family | *Some colleagues become friends | Colleagues only work at night | Colleagues never speak to you
test: Which word means a person you work with? | meeting | email | *colleague | boss
exam: «Colleagues are the people you work alongside.» Alongside means which? | Under the table | Instead of sleeping | Far away from | *Side by side
